@svenstaro I made a few updates over the weekend that may help.  Check the output of `make help-runners` and double check the paths it found.  If those look good, build the runner with `make -j 5 cuda_v12`  If that completes successfully, then build ollama with `go build .` and then try running it with `OLLAMA_DEBUG=1 ./ollama serve` 

@LeeABarron @shkron my updates over the weekend should clear that up and it should no longer expect AVX on the custom cpu flags build.  Please give it another try and let me know how it goes.

@Roger-Roger-debug that's interesting that hipcc on your system doesn't support parallelism.  I can remove that, but before I do, can you confirm it's not supported and not a possible side-effect of some other glitch?
```
% hipcc --help | grep parallel-jobs
  -parallel-jobs=<value>  Number of parallel jobs
```
I can add some additional smarts for looking in lib64 and fallback to lib if there isn't a discrete 64bit library dir under HIP_PATH.  Untill I push that change, I believe you can force it by setting GPU_LIB_DIR to the correct location to find the 64 bit libs on your system.

@shkron the failure to find the libraries during linking should be resolved now.

@chris-hatton there might be other architecture flags that are causing the illegal instruction.  Can you check your `/proc/cpuinfo` flags for `fma` and `f16c`?


As others have mentioned on this PR, please make sure to do a `make clean` if you pick up a new commit from my branch to ensure there isn't any cruft left around from prior builds.  I'm still moving things around a bit to try to cover as many scenarios as possible.

@dhiltgen I have a strange follow up question. As I mentioned in my previous comment,  I can now successfully build and run custom `cuda_v12` runner, thanks to you. So it works when I am building and running from the same local dir (e.g. ~/ollama/ollama serve)

But, when I move this binary to `/usr/bin/ollama` (that is where the previous instance has been configured) and do `ollama serve` I only get the default cpu runner and lose cuda one. I am very confused. Are there any additional configs that go with ollama binary?

What's even more strange is that when I move the binary back to the local dir, it still can't see cuda_v12 runner.

@BlastyCZ can you take a peek earlier in the logs to see what runners it detected?  `msg="Dynamic LLM libraries" runners="[...]"` - my suspicion is it's not finding the cuda_v12 runner for some reason.  Setting OLLAMA_DEBUG=1 may shed a bit more light.  If that doesn't yield a clear answer, please share the startup logs with debug on and hopefully I can spot what is going wrong.

@shkron thanks for the confirmation.  I'll get the PR updated a bit later to add the stubs dir in automatically.

To answer the question on "installation" after building, I'll need to update the docs to explain a bit more.  In short, this PR does away with the payload model we previously used where the runners were carried in the main binary and extracted on startup into /tmp.  Instead, they're kept as discrete binaries.  I've created a `make dist` target that will copy the executables, built libraries, and essential dependencies into `./dist/$OS-$ARCH/` which you can relocate wherever you'd like, as long as you retain the relative layout for the main executable and libraries/runners.
